{
  "dom_id": "step-q93929",
  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X∼N(2,{3}^{2}),$ compute $P(X<5.09).$\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,1.0\\,$ | $\\,0.8485\\,$ | $\\,0.8508\\,$ | $\\,0.8531\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.1\\,$ | $\\,0.8708\\,$ | $\\,0.8729\\,$ | $\\,0.8749\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.2\\,$ | $\\,0.8907\\,$ | $\\,0.8925\\,$ | $\\,0.8944\\,$ |",
  "worked_solution": "Given a normal random variable $X∼N(\\mu ,{\\sigma }^{2}),$ we have\n\n$$\nP(X<x)=P(Z<\\frac{x-\\mu }{\\sigma })\n$$\n\nwhere $Z∼N(0,1)$ is a standard normal random variable. In our case, we have $\\mu =2$ and $\\sigma =3.$\n\nFirst, we transform $X$ into a standard normal random variable $Z$ by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<5.09) & =P(Z<\\frac{5.09-2}{3}) \\\\  & =P(Z<1.03) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 1.03) \\\\  & =Φ(1.03)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe required probability is represented by the area shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14263116/assets/5490abcdc3725baabf243753060aaacb773aabc6013ef2f51ade7a1f1f480d26.png)\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(1.03)=0.8485.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(X<5.09)=0.8485.\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
